WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.091
Cargo security is not finished when the
first build succeeds.

2
00:00:03.171 --> 00:00:06.012
Dependencies change, advisories are
published, licenses matter,

3
00:00:06.092 --> 00:00:08.534
maintainers move on, and organizational
policy evolves.

4
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:11.903
A build that was acceptable last month may
need attention after a new advisory,

5
00:00:11.983 --> 00:00:15.521
license review, version conflict,
abandoned dependency signal, or policy update.

6
00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.993
Teams should maintain dependency
visibility over time.

7
00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:20.714
Advisory review helps identify known
vulnerability signals.

8
00:00:20.794 --> 00:00:24.233
License review helps ensure the project
can legally and operationally use what it

9
00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:24.612
ships.

10
00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:27.184
Policy checks can flag banned crates,
duplicate versions,

11
00:00:27.264 --> 00:00:30.902
risky features, maintenance concerns, or
dependencies that require extra approval.

12
00:00:30.982 --> 00:00:34.470
SBOM-style records support this work by
describing what components were shipped.

13
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.394
They do not magically make a project
secure,

14
00:00:36.474 --> 00:00:39.713
but they help teams answer urgent
questions when a dependency issue appears.

15
00:00:39.793 --> 00:00:41.487
Which applications include this crate?

16
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:42.863
Which versions were released?

17
00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:45.036
Which artifact contains the affected
component?

18
00:00:45.116 --> 00:00:46.412
Which team owns the follow-up?

19
00:00:46.492 --> 00:00:48.784
CI checks can make dependency governance
repeatable,

20
00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:50.559
but they should remain understandable.

21
00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:53.529
A policy gate that nobody understands
becomes noise or frustration.

22
00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:55.652
Good checks explain what failed, why it
matters,

23
00:00:55.732 --> 00:00:57.775
and what kind of review or exception is
expected.

24
00:00:57.855 --> 00:01:01.344
Security signals are most useful when they
lead to timely, accountable decisions.

25
00:01:01.424 --> 00:01:04.763
Exceptions should have owners, reasons,
scope, and expiration expectations.

26
00:01:04.843 --> 00:01:08.132
Sometimes a team may temporarily accept a
dependency risk while waiting for an

27
00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:09.557
upstream fix or migration path.

28
00:01:09.637 --> 00:01:11.631
That decision should be visible and
revisited.

29
00:01:11.711 --> 00:01:13.305
Dependency records, policy history,

30
00:01:13.385 --> 00:01:16.824
and SBOM data help teams respond when new
issues emerge instead of reconstructing

31
00:01:16.904 --> 00:01:18.100
the past during an incident.
